  "panel_source": "M-II (Rhythm & Groove Motor)",
  "panel_reasoning_excerpt": "### Template M7: Basal Ganglia Beat Extraction\n\n**Core claim**: The basal ganglia (particularly putamen and caudate) extract periodic temporal regularity (beat) from auditory input, automatically and obligatorily engaging the cortico-BG-thalamo-cortical motor loop even during passive listening.\n\n**Neural substrate**: Auditory cortex \u2192 putamen/caudate (beat extraction) \u2192 SMA/premotor cortex (motor prediction) \u2192 back to auditory cortex (precision-weighted temporal prediction). This loop operates automatically when periodic structure is present; it does NOT engage for non-periodic (random or complex aperiodic) temporal patterns.\n\n**Formal structure**:\n- Input: Temporal onsets from auditory cortex\n- Process: Detection of periodicity via striatal pattern matching (possibly through coincidence detection of cortical oscillatory inputs)\n- Output: Beat signal = periodic motor prediction at the detected frequency\n- PE: Events that deviate from predicted beat times \u2192 phase correction proportional to asynchrony\n\n**Key parameters**:\n- Preferred tempo range: ~60-150 BPM (1-2.5 Hz), peaking at ~120 BPM (2 Hz) \u2014 the \"resonance frequency\" of the human motor system\n- Entrainment strength: Proportional to clarity of periodic structure in input\n- Dopaminergic modulation: DA levels modulate BG sensitivity to periodic patterns (PD = impaired beat perception)\n\n**Clinical application**: Rhythmic Auditory Stimulation (Thaut) compensates for BG dysfunction by providing external beat that bypasses the damaged internal beat-extraction mechanism.\n\n**Maturity**: \u2605\u2605\u2605\u2605 (Strong neuroimaging, patient, and TMS evidence)\n\n**Key references**: Grahn & Brett (2007); Grahn & Rowe (2009, 2013); Thaut et al. (2015)",
  "justification_status": "raw_excerpt",
